============
Installing GF2X or compiling it from source
============

# This has only been tested on Ubuntu 26.04
# This is valid as of 2026, libraries may change


# To install the compiler, etc.
sudo apt install build-essential
sudo apt install m4

# To install:
sudo apt install libgf2x-dev


#######
# NOTE:
#######
# In practice it seems it is really necessary
# to compile from source and do the tuning.
# Using the libgf2x-dev package
# may result in slower runtimes by an order of magnitude
# when running factor.cpp on r=136279841


# To compile from source instead:

sudo apt install autoconf
sudo apt install libtool
sudo apt install texinfo  # needed only for makeinfo

mkdir gitlab.com
cd    gitlab.com

# It is not clear whether the gitlab repository below
# blocks access to AI bots.
# So if asking AI to look at the code,
# it may be necessary to supply a tarfile instead.

# However, when attempting to git clone with the command below,
# it appears to require a username:

git clone https://gitlab.com/gf2x/gf2x/gf2x.git/
Username for 'https://gitlab.com':


# Note: before October 2026 this was hosted at gitlab.inria.fr,
# which did block access to AI bots.
# git clone https://gitlab.inria.fr/gf2x/gf2x.git/


cd gf2x

#####################
# NOTE: make tune-fft will abort below unless these patches are applied:

patch -p1 < gf2x-1.patch
patch -p1 < gf2x-2.patch
patch -p1 < gf2x-3.patch

# if unpatched, the result of that abort is that it will just use the defaults in gf2x/gf2x-thresholds.h
# (which date from 2012)
####################

####################
# Maybe do this, to avoid a warning when running "autoreconf --install"
# Is it actually needed?

ln -s gf2x.h.in.in gf2x.h.in
####################

# as per https://libntl.org/doc/tour-gf2x.html

# CAUTION: if you make a mistake below
# (like doing "make check" before doing make,
# or attempting "make install" without "sudo")
# you will probably need to start over at the ./configure step

autoreconf --install
./configure
make
make check
make tune-lowlevel
make tune-toom
make tune-fft
# This will install in /usr/local/
sudo make install

# not necessary to limit it, doesn't save much time at all
# make tune-fft FFT_TUNING_LIMIT=4000000  # in the range of 2.13M for degree 136279841

# if trying to tinker with the tunefft.c source code, it can be compiled with:
# gcc -g -O4 -I. -Ifft -msse3 -mssse3 -msse4.1 -mpclmul -march=native -mtune=native    src/tunefft.c src/tuning-common.o src/.libs/libtiming.a ./libgf2x-local.a  -o src/tunefft

# Note:
# /usr/local/lib is NOT in the default library search path in Ubuntu
# To add it, do
echo "/usr/local/lib" | sudo tee -a /etc/ld.so.conf.d/local.conf
sudo ldconfig

# If you want to make sure the installed package doesn't interfere:
sudo apt remove libgf2x-dev


To run factor you will need:

sudo apt install libgomp1
(and the libraries compiled for GMP and NTL also)

To compile "factor"

either use a handmade Makefile, or

make -n

rm -f factor
/bin/bash ../libtool  --tag=CXX   --mode=link g++  -g -O2 -Wall -W -fopenmp  -o factor factor.o halfgcd.o   -lgmp

and change the last line to:
/bin/bash ../libtool  --tag=CXX   --mode=link g++  -g -O2 -Wall -W -fopenmp  -o factor factor.o halfgcd.o   -lntl -lgf2x -lgmp
